# =========================================================================
# core/mod — core types and error handling for AgnosAI.
#
# Parity oracle: rust-old/src/core/mod.rs
#
# The foundational types shared across every other group: agents, tasks, crews,
# messages, resources, and errors. No I/O — pure data, with JSON support via
# bayan where the oracle used serde.
#
# This module is the hub mirroring mod.rs: it carries no logic of its own, only
# the include order. That order IS a dependency constraint (unlike the learning
# hub's) and Cyrius resolution is single-pass, so callees must precede callers:
#
#   core_error     — standalone
#   core_json      — shared JSON field helpers (no oracle module; extracted once
#                    task, resource and agent all needed them)
#   core_message   — needs src/id.cyr and core_error
#   core_resource  — needs core_json
#   core_task      — needs core_json and src/id.cyr
#   core_agent     — needs core_json and core_resource
#   core_crew      — needs all of the above
#
# `src/id.cyr` must be included before this hub; src/main.cyr does that.
#
# ✅ **The `#[cfg(feature = "hwaccel")]` half of resource.rs is PORTED** — M11,
# 2026-08-10. `agnosai_accelerator_type_from_hwaccel` (:773),
# `agnosai_hw_inventory_from_hwaccel` (:797) and the whole
# `TrainingMemoryEstimate` set (:854-910) are in `core/resource.cyr`. This note
# used to list it as owed, and before that as "outside the v2.0.0 parity bar" —
# a framing that was self-issued, never a user decision, and is retired: a cargo
# feature gate is not a scope boundary.
#
# One carve-out remains, and it is the ONE the user actually set:
#   * `AgentDefinition::personality` — deferred; see docs/development/roadmap.md:43
#     for why and for the exit condition. bhava has no Cyrius port, so there is
#     nothing to depend on. The field serializes as null meanwhile, which is
#     exactly what the default Rust build emits, so the wire form matches. It is
#     the only item the user ever set aside.
#
# ## The oracle's doctest
#
# `core/mod.rs` carries the one real doctest under `rust-old/src/` — the other
# three ` ``` ` blocks are `text`, `ignore` and `yaml`, none of which `cargo test`
# compiles. It parses an `AgentDefinition` from JSON, makes a `Task` and a
# `CrewSpec`; a doctest asserts that the documented example still runs at all,
# which is a promise to the reader that outlives any given release.
#
#   agnosai_agent_from_json("{\"agent_key\":\"analyst\", \"name\":\"Analyst\",
#                            \"role\":\"data analyst\", \"goal\":\"analyze data\"}")
#   agnosai_task_new("Analyze quarterly revenue trends")
#   agnosai_crew_new("analysis-crew")
#
# ⚠ **`cyrius doctest src/core/mod.cyr` runs the block below.** An earlier draft
# of this note said "Cyrius has no doctest runner" and pinned the example as
# assertions instead. That was **wrong** — `cyrius doctest <file.cyr>` exists and
# executes the `# >>>` lines, checking the result against `# ===`. Verify a
# claimed toolchain gap against bare `cyrius` before writing around it.
#
# ⚠ **`cyrius doctest` auto-prepends NOTHING** — not `[deps].stdlib`, not the
# `[deps.NAME]` bundles, not even `lib/str.cyr`. Measured: a doctest calling
# `str_from` in this project reports `undefined function 'str_from'`. That is why
# 24 `include` lines precede two lines of actual example. `cyrius tests` and
# `cyrius bench` DO prepend, so the habit built on `.tcyr` files does not carry
# over, and the failure is a compile error rather than anything that names the
# real cause. cyrius's own `lib/hashmap.cyr` doctest is broken by exactly this —
# it includes `lib/string.cyr` where it needs `lib/str.cyr` — and is filed
# upstream.
#
# **Keep the include list in step with `src/main.cyr`.** If a new `[deps]` entry
# becomes reachable from `core/`, this block breaks and `check-clean.sh` will say
# so. `tests/core_mod_doctest.tcyr` remains alongside it: a doctest can only
# check an exit code, and the suite checks the fields.
#
# >>> include "lib/syscalls.cyr"
# >>> include "lib/alloc.cyr"
# >>> include "lib/result.cyr"
# >>> include "lib/fmt.cyr"
# >>> include "lib/io.cyr"
# >>> include "lib/str.cyr"
# >>> include "lib/string.cyr"
# >>> include "lib/vec.cyr"
# >>> include "lib/slice.cyr"
# >>> include "lib/hashmap.cyr"
# >>> include "lib/chrono.cyr"
# >>> include "lib/args.cyr"
# >>> include "lib/fs.cyr"
# >>> include "lib/process.cyr"
# >>> include "lib/atomic.cyr"
# >>> include "lib/sync.cyr"
# >>> include "lib/thread.cyr"
# >>> include "lib/random.cyr"
# >>> include "lib/sha1.cyr"
# >>> include "lib/math.cyr"
# >>> include "lib/bayan.cyr"
# >>> include "lib/sakshi.cyr"
# >>> include "lib/ai-hwaccel.cyr"
# >>> include "src/units.cyr"
# >>> include "src/order.cyr"
# >>> include "src/id.cyr"
# >>> include "src/core/mod.cyr"
# >>> var _J = "{\"agent_key\":\"analyst\",\"name\":\"Analyst\",";
# >>> var _K = "\"role\":\"data analyst\",\"goal\":\"analyze data\"}";
# >>> fn main() {
# >>>     alloc_init();
# >>>     var j = str_cat(str_from(_J), str_from(_K));
# >>>     if (agnosai_agent_from_json(j) == 0) { return 1; }
# >>>     if (agnosai_task_new(str_from("Analyze quarterly revenue trends")) == 0) { return 2; }
# >>>     if (agnosai_crew_new(str_from("analysis-crew")) == 0) { return 3; }
# >>>     return 0;
# >>> }
# >>> var r = main(); syscall(60, r);
# === 0
# =========================================================================

include "src/core/error.cyr"
include "src/core/json.cyr"
include "src/core/message.cyr"
include "src/core/resource.cyr"
include "src/core/task.cyr"
include "src/core/agent.cyr"
include "src/core/crew.cyr"
